  "body": "← Experiments\nHow does changing the starting angle and length of a pendulum affect its swing period?\nExperiment options\n45%\n?\nFind content\n0 m\n5 m\n10 m\n15 m\n20 m\n5.00 m · 26.0° from vertical\nDashed arc: starting swing. Same metre scale as you change length.\nDrawing scale\nTime for one full swing\n4.564\ns\nAngle\n25.955\ndeg\nPlayback\nPause\nRunning\nPendulum length\nm\n0.1\n20\n\nLonger rods swing more slowly.\n\nGravity\nm/s²\n0.1\n30\nStarting angle\ndeg\n0\n90\n\nRelease angle (0–90°). Larger swings take longer.\n\nAdvance motion by 0.1 seconds\nRestart activity\nRestart motion\nElapsed time\n0.383\ns\nCompleted cycles\n0\nLast complete period\nUnavailable\ns\nHorizontal position\n2.188\nm\nVertical position\n-4.496\nm\nAngle\nAngle (degrees)\n\nScroll the plot to inspect its full scale, or open the observations below.\n\n-40\n-20\n0\n20\n40\n0\n2.282\n4.564\n6.846\n9.128\nModel time (s)\nAngle · solid\nView observations\n\nIdeal rigid-rod pendulum released at rest, starting angle 0–90°. No friction or air resistance.\nNonlinear motion solves θ″=−(g/L)sinθ using the elliptic time coordinate. The calculated period is 4√(L/g)K(sin(amplitude/2)); motion is independent of frame rate. Changing length, gravity or starting angle restarts the run.\nComparisons use shared gravity and initial amplitude. Length is the independent variable.\n\n💡 Ideas\nApply\n↑",
